to understand ratios you just need to  understand this simple story Gaurav and  J used to receive chocolates from a  mysterious magic hand every day but the  magic hand was a little biased to us J  every time it gave two chocolates to  Gaurav it gave three - J - to Gaurav and  three to J nobody knew why of course J  did not complain so the ratio of the  number of chocolates Gaurav got to the  number of chocolates J got is written as  2 : 3 or 2/3 this can be read as 2 is to  3 we are basically comparing the number  of chocolates God of God to the number  of chocolates J got this went on for  quite a few days so every time the magic  hand gave two chocolates to Gaurav it  gave 3 chocolates - J now let's count  the total number of chocolates  each of them have Gaurav has 4 and J has  6 the ratio now becomes 4 by 6 the magic  hand again gives God of 2 chocolates and  j-3 chocolates now the total number of  chocolates with Gaurav is 6 and the  total number of chocolates with J is 9  so the ratio becomes 6 by 9 both these  ratios when simplified will give us 2 by  3 in every case the ratio has remained  the same what does this mean if we  multiply 2 with 2 and 3 also with 2 we  get 4 by 6 if we multiply 2 with 3 and 3  with 3 we will get 6 by 9 and similarly  if you multiply 2 with 4 and 3 with 4 we  will get 8 by 12 which is again  equivalent to 2 by 3 so all the three  ratios are equal to 2 by 3 what does all  this mean it means that if the ratio of  two quantities is 2 by 3 then the  quantities will be multiples of 2 and  multiples of 3 respectively  and not necessarily just two and three a  few students have this confusion that  the ratio of two by three will mean  Gaurav has just two chocolates and  judges three chocolates if the ratio is  2 by 3 then the quantities will be in  the form of 2x and 3x where 2x and 3x  are multiples of 2 and 3 so ratio is  basically a comparison of quantities but  there is a bit more to comparison of  quantities we see that next
 

